151
00:08:16.883 --> 00:08:17.822
Part 6.

152
00:08:18.102 --> 00:08:21.025
Lessons the tests taught (they apply to interviews too).

153
00:08:22.275 --> 00:08:23.149
Question.

154
00:08:23.429 --> 00:08:27.483
Why did a 200 ms quorum timeout make the first write fail?

155
00:08:27.763 --> 00:08:29.128
Thirty-second answer:

156
00:08:29.408 --> 00:08:43.637
The first request hits a cold JVM (servlet initialisation, JIT, new HTTP connections) and all three replica calls exceeded 200 ms, so the coordinator correctly answered 503:

157
00:08:43.917 --> 00:08:45.219
0 or 2 acks.

158
00:08:45.499 --> 00:08:54.075
Timeouts must cover p99 latency including warm-up, and production services warm up (or fail readiness) before taking traffic.

159
00:08:54.355 --> 00:08:56.211
The test profile uses 2 s.

160
00:08:57.561 --> 00:08:58.435
Question.

161
00:08:58.715 --> 00:09:01.851
Why did read repair sometimes find nothing to repair?

162
00:09:02.131 --> 00:09:03.497
Thirty-second answer:

163
00:09:03.777 --> 00:09:13.483
A write returns after W acks, but the other N minus W requests are still in flight and can land later, so the stale replica had already caught up.

164
00:09:13.763 --> 00:09:16.281
That is how Dynamo-style stores converge.

165
00:09:16.561 --> 00:09:25.478
The coordinator now exposes an inFlight() count (a good gauge to export), and the test waits for it to reach zero before failing a replica.

166
00:09:26.828 --> 00:09:27.703
Question.

167
00:09:27.983 --> 00:09:32.783
Why did invalid query parameters return 500 instead of 400?

168
00:09:33.063 --> 00:09:34.428
Thirty-second answer:

169
00:09:34.708 --> 00:09:44.585
A class-level @Validated makes Spring wrap the controller in a validation proxy that throws ConstraintViolationException (500 unless you map it).

170
00:09:44.865 --> 00:09:55.980
Without it, Spring MVC's built-in method validation (Framework 6.1 plus) validates @RequestParam constraints and returns a 400 ProblemDetail.

171
00:09:57.330 --> 00:09:58.205
Question.

172
00:09:58.485 --> 00:10:01.130
Why are Snowflake ids returned as strings?

173
00:10:01.410 --> 00:10:02.775
Thirty-second answer:

174
00:10:03.055 --> 00:10:12.911
JavaScript numbers are doubles: integers above 2^53 lose precision, and a 63-bit Snowflake id would be silently corrupted in a browser.

